{"type":"tool_result","timestamp":"2026-09-26T15:24:40.033Z","tool_id":"tooluse_pxmbplPDfAsK0nP630IyyW","status":"success","output":"Contents of file docs\\README.md:\n\n1 | # SQLFluff - Generating the document website\n2 | \n3 | You can run the following steps to generate the documentation website:\n4 | \n5 | ```\n6 | tox -e docbuild,doclinting\n7 | ```\n8 | \n9 | The `docbuild` job will recognise when source files have changed and only\n10 | generate the changed files. To force a clean build (for example when changing\n11 | config) rather than the source files use the following command from the project\n12 | root directory (drop the `-C docs` if running from within the `docs` directory).\n13 | \n14 | ```\n15 | make -C docs clean\n16 | ```\n17 | \n18 | The built HTML should be placed in `docs/build/html` and can be opened directly\n19 | in the browser or you can launch a simple webserver with the below command\n20 | and then navigate to http://127.0.0.1:8000/ to view the site locally:\n21 | \n22 | ```\n23 | python -m http.server --directory docs/build/html\n24 | ```\n25 | \n26 | Again, this command is run from the root server, not the `docs` subfolder but you\n27 | can alter the path as appropriate if needs be.\n28 | \n29 | If you don't want to use `tox`, then you can complete the steps manually with\n30 | the following commands after setting up your Python environment as detailed\n31 | in the [CONTRIBUTING.md](../CONTRIBUTING.md) file.\n32 | \n33 | ```\n34 | cd docs\n35 | pip install -r requirements.txt\n36 | make html\n37 | python -m http.server --directory build/html\n38 | ```\n39 | \n40 | Or alternatively from the root folder:\n41 | \n42 | ```\n43 | pip install -r docs/requirements.txt\n44 | make -C docs html\n45 | python -m http.server --directory docs/build/html\n46 | ```\n47 | \n48 | The docs use Sphinx and are generated from the source code.\n49 | The config is available in `docs/source/conf.py`.\n50 | "}
